Day 3: Determination of Loss on Ignition (LOI) in Limestone-Type Standard Samples According to O‘z DSt 0214:2003 and Study of the Reaction Engineering of Acid Digestion and Leaching Processes
Objective of the Practical Training
The objective of the practical training was to familiarize students with the methodology for determining Loss on Ignition (LOI) in accordance with O‘z DSt 0214:2003 under laboratory conditions, to develop their skills in analytical calculations, and to study the theoretical and practical aspects of acid digestion and leaching processes.
Activities Performed
Stage 1. Theoretical Preparation
At the initial stage of the practical training, students studied the requirements and provisions of O‘z DSt 0214:2003. The theoretical principles of determining Loss on Ignition (LOI), calculation procedures, and standard formulas were examined in detail. Students analyzed the physical meaning of the variables involved in the calculation formula and practiced solving sample problems related to LOI determination.
Stage 2. Laboratory Analysis of Standard Samples
In accordance with the standard procedure, students analyzed two certified reference samples, СН-2 and СН-3. The sample masses, masses before ignition, and masses after ignition were determined experimentally, and the percentage of mass loss during ignition was calculated using the standard formula.
Sample	Sample Mass (g)	Mass Before Ignition (g)	Mass After Ignition (g)	LOI (%)
СН-2	0.5001	21.6402	21.4568	36.67
СН-3	0.5002	17.5538	17.3955	31.64
The calculations showed that the Loss on Ignition values were 36.67% for sample СН-2 and 31.64% for sample СН-3.
Stage 3. Comparison with Certified Reference Values
The experimental results were compared with the certified values of the reference materials (GSO).
Sample	Measured Value (%)	Certified Value (GSO, %)
СН-2	36.67	31.93 ± 0.85
СН-3	31.64	31.92 ± 0.85
The comparison demonstrated that the result obtained for sample СН-3 was in close agreement with the certified reference value. In contrast, sample СН-2 exhibited a deviation of approximately 4.74% above the certified value. Students discussed possible sources of this discrepancy, including weighing errors, deviations in the ignition procedure, and sample heterogeneity.
Stage 4. Investigation of the Ignition Process
During the practical work, students acquired knowledge and practical skills related to:
•	the ignition of samples within the temperature range of 950–1050 °C; 
•	cooling procedures using a desiccator and subsequent weighing using an analytical balance; 
•	calculation methods for determining mass loss during ignition; 
•	application of correction factors in the presence of iron, fluorine, and sulfur impurities. 
Stage 5. Evaluation of Measurement Accuracy
Students evaluated the reliability and precision of the obtained results using the quality-control procedures specified in the standard. It was established that, for the measurement range of 30–40%, the permissible relative deviation between two measurements should not exceed 3.9%. The control equation was studied and applied to assess the agreement between primary and control measurements.
Study of Acid Digestion and Leaching Reaction Engineering
In the second part of the practical training, students studied the reaction engineering aspects of acid digestion and leaching processes used in mineral processing.
The following activities were performed:
•	analysis of acid digestion as a batch reactor process; 
•	investigation of the effects of reagent concentration, temperature, and mixing intensity on the dissolution rate of mineral matrices; 
•	examination of reaction stoichiometry for target minerals; 
•	calculation of the excess acid required to achieve complete conversion of the mineral phase; 
•	analysis of mass-transfer phenomena and reaction kinetics during acid digestion and leaching operations. 
Knowledge and Skills Acquired
As a result of the practical training, students gained competencies in:
•	working with the requirements of O‘z DSt 0214:2003; 
•	determination of Loss on Ignition using the ignition method; 
•	accurate weighing procedures using analytical balances; 
•	performing analytical and laboratory calculations; 
•	comparing experimental results with certified reference values; 
•	evaluating measurement accuracy and reliability; 
•	stoichiometric calculations for acid digestion processes; 
•	analysis of factors influencing chemical reactor performance and dissolution kinetics. 
Conclusion
During the practical training, students successfully mastered the methodology for determining Loss on Ignition (LOI) in limestone-type samples according to O‘z DSt 0214:2003. The results indicated that sample СН-3 closely matched the certified reference value, whereas sample СН-2 exhibited a significant positive deviation. Furthermore, students acquired theoretical and practical knowledge of acid digestion and leaching processes, including reaction stoichiometry, reagent consumption calculations, and the influence of process parameters on reaction efficiency. The practical training contributed significantly to the development of professional competencies in analytical chemistry, mineral processing, and chemical technology.

Day 4 : Solution Thermodynamics: Study of the Behavior of Metal Ions in Concentrated Acids, Activity Coefficients, and Solubility Limits

Objective of the Practical Training

The primary objective of the practical training was to strengthen students’ understanding of solution thermodynamics, investigate the behavior of metal ions in concentrated acidic media, analyze the influence of activity coefficients and solubility limits on analytical processes, and develop skills for assessing the risk of precipitation in liquid samples prior to instrumental analysis.

Activities Performed
Stage 1. Theoretical Preparation

During the initial stage of the practical training, students studied the fundamental principles of solution thermodynamics. Particular attention was given to the chemical behavior of metal ions in concentrated acidic solutions, ion–ion interactions, and the influence of ionic strength on solution properties.

The following topics were examined:

concepts of concentration and activity;
physical and chemical significance of activity coefficients;
effects of increasing ionic strength on activity coefficients;
solubility equilibria and solubility product constants;
stability of metal ions in acidic media.
Stage 2. Investigation of Metal Ion Behavior in Concentrated Acids

Students examined the behavior of metal ions in concentrated sulfuric, nitric, and hydrochloric acid solutions. The existence of metal species as free ions, complex ions, and associated particles was analyzed.

During the practical session, the influence of the following factors on the stability of metal ions was evaluated:

solution pH;
acid concentration;
ionic strength;
temperature;
presence of complexing agents.

Students determined that changes in solution composition may lead to the precipitation of certain metals in the form of hydroxides, sulfates, or other sparingly soluble compounds.

Stage 3. Assessment of Precipitation Risk

In this stage, students evaluated the likelihood of precipitation occurring in liquid samples prior to instrumental analysis. The assessment was carried out by considering solution composition, ion concentrations, and equilibrium conditions.

The following risk factors for precipitation were identified:

reduction in solubility caused by dilution;
increase in pH value;
cooling of the solution;
interactions between dissolved ions;
exceeding the saturation limit of the solution.

Students learned the importance of controlling these parameters during sample preparation to ensure reliable analytical results.

Stage 4. Development of Strategies for Preventing Precipitation

Students analyzed various methods for minimizing precipitation and developed practical recommendations for maintaining solution stability.

The main strategies proposed included:

maintaining adequate acidity in the solution;
storing samples in sealed containers prior to analysis;
controlling storage and operating temperatures;
filtering samples to remove suspended particles;
using complexing agents to stabilize dissolved metal ions.

Particular emphasis was placed on the role of complex-forming reagents in preventing precipitation and preserving analyte concentrations in solution.

Stage 5. Study of Complexing Agents and Their Applications

Students investigated the function of complexing agents in maintaining metal ions in dissolved form.

The following reagents were considered as examples:

EDTA (ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid);
citrate ions;
tartrate ions;
ammonia-based complexes.

It was demonstrated that these reagents form stable coordination complexes with metal ions, thereby reducing the probability of precipitation and improving the accuracy and reliability of instrumental analytical measurements.

Knowledge and Skills Acquired

Upon completion of the practical training, students acquired competencies in:

fundamental principles of solution thermodynamics;
practical application of activity coefficients;
behavior of metal ions in acidic solutions;
evaluation of solubility equilibria;
assessment of precipitation risks in analytical samples;
preparation of liquid samples for instrumental analysis;
application of complexing agents in analytical chemistry;
methods for improving the reliability and accuracy of laboratory results.
Conclusion

During the practical training, students reinforced their understanding of solution thermodynamics through both theoretical study and practical analysis. The behavior of metal ions in concentrated acidic solutions, the significance of activity coefficients, and the impact of solubility limits on analytical procedures were thoroughly examined. In addition, students assessed precipitation risks in liquid samples prior to instrumental analysis and developed effective strategies for preventing precipitation, including the use of complexing agents. As a result, the training contributed significantly to the development of professional competencies in analytical chemistry, hydrometallurgy, and chemical technology.

