1. Introduction
On June 15, 2026, students of Samarkand International University of Technology began their industrial internship at the drilling sites operated by ERIELL. This visit marked the first day of their practical training. The main purpose of the internship was to introduce students to real industrial working conditions, drilling equipment, safety requirements, and the general organisation of work at a drilling site.
The students were accompanied and supervised during the visit. Upon arrival, they became familiar with the site environment and observed the main facilities and equipment used in drilling operations.
2. Purpose of the First-Day Practice
The purpose of the first day was not to involve students directly in technical operations, but to provide them with an introductory understanding of the drilling site and its working process.
The main objectives were:
to introduce students to the structure of a land drilling rig;
to familiarise them with the main safety rules at the site;
to explain the role of personal protective equipment;
to observe the general organisation of drilling operations;
to help students connect theoretical knowledge with real industrial practice;
to develop an initial understanding of professional discipline in the oil and gas industry.
3. Site Familiarisation
During the first day, the students were introduced to the drilling site and its surrounding working area. They observed the main drilling rig, support facilities, technical equipment, tanks, auxiliary machinery, and access roads.
Special attention was given to the drilling rig structure. The students were shown the main visible parts of the rig, including the mast, drilling platform, lifting system, support units, and other site equipment. They were informed that the drilling rig is a complex engineering system used for drilling oil and gas wells, lowering and lifting drilling pipes, circulating drilling fluid, and controlling the well during the drilling process.
The students also learned that a drilling site is a high-risk industrial facility where every action must be performed according to strict safety procedures and under the supervision of responsible specialists.
4. Safety Briefing
A safety briefing was an important part of the first day. The students were informed about the basic safety requirements that must be followed at the drilling site.
The following points were explained to them:
entry to the drilling site is allowed only with permission;
students must not move around the site without supervision;
personal protective equipment must be worn at all times;
special safety boots, helmets, protective clothing, gloves, and reflective vests are required;
it is prohibited to approach operating machinery without permission;
students must keep a safe distance from lifting operations, rotating equipment, and high-pressure systems;
taking photos or videos may be restricted in certain areas;
all instructions from site supervisors and safety officers must be followed strictly.
The students were reminded that industrial safety is the first priority at any drilling site. They were also informed that failure to follow safety rules may lead to serious accidents or restriction from entering the operational area.
5. Observations Made by Students
During the visit, students observed the general working environment of a drilling site. They saw how different technical units are arranged around the rig and how the site is organised for safe and continuous operation.
They paid attention to the following aspects:
the height and structure of the drilling mast;
the role of lifting equipment in handling drilling pipes;
the presence of tanks and support systems used in drilling operations;
the movement of vehicles and machinery around the site;
the importance of communication between workers;
the necessity of discipline and teamwork in field conditions;
the importance of proper safety clothing and equipment.
The students understood that drilling operations require cooperation between engineers, drillers, mechanics, electricians, geologists, mud engineers, safety officers, and other specialists.
6. Educational Value of the Visit
The first day of practice was useful for students because it allowed them to see real industrial equipment and working conditions. Many concepts that students study theoretically at university became clearer after observing the drilling site in practice.
The visit helped students understand that drilling is not only a mechanical process, but also a combination of geology, engineering, safety management, equipment control, environmental responsibility, and teamwork.
The students also realised the importance of professional behaviour, punctuality, communication, and discipline in industrial environments.
7. Conclusion
The first day of the industrial internship at the ERIELL drilling site was successfully completed. The students became familiar with the general structure of the drilling site, observed the main drilling rig and auxiliary equipment, and received initial information about safety requirements and working procedures.
This introductory day created a strong foundation for the next stages of the internship. In the following days, students are expected to gain deeper knowledge about drilling operations, equipment functions, safety procedures, and the responsibilities of different specialists working at the site.
Overall, the first day was informative and beneficial for the students. It helped them connect their academic knowledge with real industrial practice and increased their understanding of the oil and gas drilling sector.

1. Introduction

On the second day of the industrial internship, the students of Samarkand International University of Technology continued their practical training at the drilling site. After the introductory familiarisation on the first day, the second day was focused on gaining a deeper understanding of the drilling area, the main drilling equipment, the organisation of work, and the general operational processes at the site.

The students visited the drilling area under supervision and became more familiar with how drilling operations are carried out in real industrial conditions. They observed the working environment, the arrangement of equipment, and the role of different technical systems involved in the drilling process.

2. Purpose of the Second-Day Practice

The main purpose of the second day was to help students understand the practical aspects of drilling operations and the functioning of drilling equipment.

The key objectives of the day were:

to become familiar with the drilling area and its main operational zones;
to observe the main drilling equipment and its functions;
to understand how work is organised at a drilling site;
to learn about the basic stages of the drilling process;
to observe the interaction between workers, engineers, and technical specialists;
to strengthen students’ understanding of safety requirements in the drilling area;
to connect theoretical knowledge from university courses with real field practice.
3. Familiarisation with the Drilling Area

During the second day, the students were introduced more closely to the drilling area. They observed the main operational parts of the site and learned how different zones are organised for safe and efficient work.

The students were shown the drilling rig, the working platform, support equipment, technical containers, tanks, lifting systems, and other auxiliary facilities. They learned that each part of the site has a specific purpose and that proper organisation is essential for maintaining safety and productivity.

Special attention was paid to the fact that the drilling area is a high-risk zone. Therefore, all movements and observations were carried out under the guidance of responsible specialists. The students were reminded that they must follow all instructions, keep a safe distance from operating machinery, and wear personal protective equipment at all times.

4. Study of Drilling Equipment

One of the main parts of the second-day practice was learning about the drilling equipment and its working principles. The students observed the structure and operation of the drilling rig and were informed about the role of its main components.

They became familiar with the following equipment and systems:

the drilling mast, which is used for lifting and lowering drilling pipes;
the drilling platform, where the main operational activities are carried out;
the lifting system, which helps handle heavy drilling tools and pipes;
drilling pipes, which are used to transmit rotation and drilling fluid into the well;
mud tanks and circulation systems, which are used for drilling fluid preparation and circulation;
pumps, which provide pressure for circulating drilling fluid;
auxiliary machinery and cranes used for support operations;
control and safety systems used to monitor the drilling process.

The students learned that drilling equipment works as one integrated system. Each unit has its own task, but all parts must function together to ensure continuous and safe drilling operations.

5. Understanding the Drilling Process

During the practice, the students were introduced to the general process of drilling a well. They learned that drilling is a complex engineering operation that includes several important stages.

The students were informed that the drilling process usually includes:

preparation of the drilling site;
installation and inspection of drilling equipment;
lowering drilling pipes into the well;
rotating the drilling bit to break underground rock formations;
