1. Morning Session – Visit to NEEPU "Wenwu" History Museum
The morning of July 1 was dedicated to a comprehensive tour of the NEEPU Wenwu History Museum (文武校史馆), a newly built facility that officially opened on September 7, 2019, after preparations that began in June 2014 . Located in the East Campus's No. 4 Teaching Building, the museum spans over 2,000 square meters and consists of nine thematic sections, including "Embryo and Startup," "Consolidation and Improvement," "Endeavor with Reform and Opening-up," and "Leaping Development and Rapid Progress" .
Historical Journey of the University
The students traced the university's remarkable journey through 70+ years of history. The institution was first established in Shenyang in 1949 as Shenyang Electrical Machinery Advanced Vocational School. It was relocated to Changchun in August 1949 and renamed several times before moving to Jilin in 1955, where it was designated as Jilin Electric Power School . In 1958, it was upgraded to a higher education institution and named Jilin Electric Power Institute .
Highlights of the Museum Exhibition
The museum's collection of over 10,000 photographs and nearly 1,000 cultural relics provided a vivid narrative of the university's evolution . Key exhibits that captured the students' attention included:
•	Gifts and tributes presented by the first graduating class and the first president;
•	Original 1949 news reports documenting the founding of the institution;
•	The first laboratory equipment used in the early days of teaching and research;
•	Electrical equipment built in 1937 – among the earliest teaching tools;
•	Photographs from the first graduation ceremony in 1956;
•	Historical statistics showing that by 1962, the university had 34 academic groups with 1,018 students;
•	Interactive touchscreens displaying old teaching equipment and materials from past decades.
The students also learned about the university's name changes: in 1978, it was renamed Northeast Electric Power College (having previously been Jilin Electric Power College), and finally, in 2005, it received its current name – Northeast Electric Power University . A video presentation showcased student life between 1978 and 1982, offering a glimpse into campus culture during the early reform era.
Academic Milestones and National Contributions
The museum highlighted significant academic achievements, including the first international students receiving doctoral degrees in 1982. The exhibition also emphasized how NEEPU has actively engaged in the country's national energy strategy, contributing to major power engineering projects across China .
A separate exhibition room was dedicated to the university's Dragon Boat Team, which has participated in over 180 international competitions and won 30 trophies – a source of great pride for the university community.
Current University Profile
The students learned that NEEPU currently offers 51 bachelor's degree programs, along with multiple master's and doctoral degree programs, and has a student body of approximately 25,000 students . The museum visit concluded with a deep appreciation for the university's transformation from a small vocational school to a comprehensive power engineering institution.
2. Afternoon Session – Visit to Jilin City Museum
In the afternoon, the students visited the Jilin City Museum (吉林市博物馆), a prestigious national first-class museum located on Jilin Avenue, near Century Square . Established in 1962, the museum combines natural sciences, humanities, and local art, with a collection of over 25,000 artifacts, including 1,001珍贵文物 .
The museum features eight permanent exhibition halls :
•	Jilin Meteorite Rain Exhibition – the museum's signature display;
•	Niu Zihou and Peking Opera Art Exhibition;
•	Liu Naizhong Art Gallery;
•	Songhua Lake Driftwood Root Carving Art Exhibition;
•	Jilin City Historical Exhibition (ancient and modern sections);
•	Jilin City Industrial Development Exhibition;
•	Jilin City Urban Construction Exhibition;
•	Jin Yian Art Gallery.
The students explored the rich history of Jilin City, from its origins as a prehistoric settlement through its development as a modern industrial center. The exhibitions showcased archaeological finds from the Xituanshan culture, Fuyu culture, and Bohai Kingdom periods, providing cultural context to complement their engineering studies .
3. Visit to Jilin Zoo (Jiangnan Park)
Following the museum, the students visited the nearby Jilin Zoo, located within Jiangnan Park (江南公园) – a historic urban garden established in 1908 during the Qing Dynasty . The park covers 360,000 square meters and integrates botanical gardens, classical landscapes, and a zoo .
The zoo houses over 30 species of animals, with nearly 400 individual animals, including Siberian tigers, African lions, brown bears, sika deer, peacocks, and red-crowned cranes . The students enjoyed observing these animals and appreciating the park's natural beauty, which offered a relaxing break from their intensive academic program.
4. Special Evening Exhibition – Jilin No. 1 Meteorite (16:30 Session)
Some students returned to the Jilin City Museum for the 16:30 session of the "Jilin No. 1 Meteorite" exhibition . This exhibition is the only public museum in China dedicated exclusively to meteorites .
The exhibition tells the story of the Jilin meteorite shower – a spectacular cosmic event that occurred on March 8, 1976, when a meteorite shower fell over northern Jilin City . The largest fragment, Jilin No. 1, weighs an astonishing 1,770 kilograms and is recognized as the heaviest known stony meteorite in the world .
The exhibition hall covers 2,060 square meters and is divided into sections: "Meteorites and the Universe," "Meteorites and Earth," "The Jilin Meteorite Shower," and "Meteorites and Humanity" . Students passed through a "time tunnel" and explored interactive displays combining sound, light, and multimedia effects. The exhibition also features a planetarium theater that simulates the meteorite's dramatic entry into Earth's atmosphere .
This unique experience left a lasting impression on the students, combining scientific wonder with a tangible connection to the cosmic forces that have shaped our planet.
5. Summary and Reflections
July 1 was a day of profound historical and cultural discovery. The morning visit to NEEPU's history museum allowed students to understand the deep institutional roots and engineering heritage of their host university, while the afternoon and evening excursions provided broader insights into Jilin's regional history, natural beauty, and even its connection to the wider universe.
The students returned to campus with a deeper appreciation for both the academic institution hosting them and the cultural landscape of Jilin City. The day successfully balanced educational enrichment with leisure, reinforcing the summer camp's mission of fostering cross-cultural understanding and holistic learning.

1. Morning Session (9:30 AM) – Lecture on "Principles of Nuclear Power Plants and Introduction to Major System Equipment"
The morning session was a comprehensive lecture delivered by Professor Wang Shenglong from the School of Energy and Power Engineering at NEEPU. The session provided students with a thorough understanding of nuclear power generation, from fundamental principles to system components and safety mechanisms. The presentation slides provided detailed visual references that greatly enhanced the learning experience.
Fundamental Principles of Nuclear Power Generation
The lecturer began by establishing the core distinction between nuclear and thermal power generation. While both systems ultimately generate electricity through turbine-driven generators, the key difference lies in the heat source: nuclear plants use a nuclear reactor instead of a boiler, and there is no combustion or chemical reaction – only nuclear fission . Consequently, nuclear power plants have no smokestacks, as they do not produce flue gases.
The lecturer explained that nuclear energy is converted into thermal energy, which then produces steam, and finally the steam's kinetic energy is converted into electrical energy through a turbine and generator. The theoretical foundation involves neutrons moving at the speed of light to initiate fission reactions, with the most common fissile material being Uranium-235. The basic fission reaction was presented as:
U235 + n → products + Q + n (where Q represents the energy released)
Types of Nuclear Reactions
The lecture covered two primary types of nuclear reactions:
•	Fission reactions – splitting heavy nuclei to release energy (used in current nuclear power plants);
•	Fusion reactions – combining light nuclei (still under research);
•	Chain fission reactions – the self-sustaining process where neutrons released from one fission event trigger subsequent fissions .
Pressurized Water Reactor (PWR) Technology
The lecturer focused on the Pressurized Water Reactor (PWR) , which is the most widely used reactor type globally. The presentation slides illustrated:
•	Nuclear energy → Thermal energy conversion within the reactor core;
•	Fuel assemblies containing U-235 in the form of ceramic pellets;
•	Control rods and their drive mechanisms for regulating the chain reaction;
•	Coolant (water) circulating through the core to absorb heat and transfer it to the steam generator .
The chain fission reaction is controlled by absorbing neutrons using either:
•	Solid-state methods (control rods made of materials like boron or cadmium);
•	Liquid-state methods (soluble neutron absorbers in the coolant).
Key Components and Systems
The lecturer systematically introduced the major equipment of a nuclear power plant:
a) Reactor Core (Active Zone)
The "heart" of the reactor, containing :
•	Nuclear fuel assemblies – fuel rods arranged in a 15×15 or 17×17 configuration;
•	Moderator – typically water, to slow down neutrons for thermal fission (PWRs are thermal neutron reactors);
•	Control materials and control rod assemblies – to regulate neutron population and reaction rate;
•	Burnable poisons and soluble poisons – for long-term reactivity control;
•	Coolant – to absorb heat and carry it out of the core.
b) Reactor Pressure Vessel and Internals
The presentation showed detailed cross-sections of a typical PWR pressure vessel, containing the core and supporting structures, designed to withstand high pressure and temperature while maintaining integrity .
c) Pressurizer
This component maintains the primary circuit at the required pressure to prevent boiling. The slides referenced Shanghai Boiler Works as a major manufacturer.
d) Steam Generator
A critical component that transfers heat from the primary circuit (radioactive water) to the secondary circuit (clean water) without mixing them. The slide showed U-shaped heat transfer tubes and a steam-water separator. The lecturer emphasized that the steam generator does not allow radioactive water to mix with clear water – a vital safety feature.
e) Reactor Coolant Pumps (Main Pumps)
Circulate the pressurized water through the primary circuit to remove heat from the core.
f) Turbine-Generator Set
The presentation showed several examples of Chinese nuclear turbines:
•	Qinshan Phase II Unit 3 – the first fully domestic 650 MW nuclear steam turbine;
•	Ling Ao NPP Unit 1;
•	Sanmen NPP Unit 1 turbine high-pressure cylinder;
•	Fuqing NPP Unit 1 turbine high/intermediate-pressure cylinder.
g) Condenser
Cools and condenses the exhaust steam from the turbine, returning it to the feedwater system.
Three Circuits of a Nuclear Power Plant
The lecturer explained the three-circuit design that ensures nuclear safety:
•	First circuit (Primary) – pressurized water circulates through the reactor core, absorbing heat and becoming radioactive. This circuit includes the reactor, pressurizer, steam generator primary side, and main pumps.
•	Second circuit (Secondary) – clean water is converted to steam in the steam generator. This steam drives the turbine and then is condensed in the condenser.
•	Third circuit (Cooling) – uses water from an external source (river, sea, or cooling towers) to remove waste heat from the condenser.
Safety and Social Responsibility
A significant portion of the lecture addressed nuclear safety and the multiple barriers designed to contain radiation:
The Four Barriers Against Radiation Release:
1.	Fuel pellet matrix – retains most fission products within the ceramic UO₂ pellets;
2.	Fuel rod cladding – hermetically sealed zirconium alloy tubes prevent fission product release into the coolant;
3.	Primary circuit pressure boundary – the reactor coolant system forms a closed, leak-tight barrier;
4.	Containment building – a reinforced concrete structure (typically 1 km exclusion zone) that provides the ultimate barrier against external events and internal accidents .
The lecturer emphasized that reactor power output must be controlled, with material changes (replacing partially spent uranium fuel) occurring during refueling cycles.
Nuclear Power Plants in China – A Showcase
The lecturer presented images and information on China's major nuclear power plants, demonstrating the country's nuclear development path :
•	Qinshan Nuclear Power Plant – China's first independently designed and constructed NPP, starting operation in 1991 ;
•	Daya Bay Nuclear Power Plant – the first large-scale commercial NPP, completed in 1994 through international cooperation ;
•	Ling'ao NPP – with units based on the Daya Bay "duplication policy" ;
•	Tianwan NPP – a cooperation project ;
•	Hualong One (HPR1000) – the third-generation PWR independently developed by China, incorporating 30 years of design and operation experience ;
•	Fuqing NPP – home to Hualong One demonstration units ;
•	Sanmen NPP – featuring advanced turbine technology .
The presentation included images of the Hualong One containment dome construction, showcasing the double-layered containment design that ensures integrity even under extreme conditions .

2. Afternoon Session (2:00 PM) – Chinese Culture Class: "Colorful Traditional Chinese Festivals"
The afternoon session was dedicated to Chinese tea culture, offering students both theoretical knowledge and hands-on experience. The teacher skillfully wove together history, literature, and practical skills.
Opening: The Seven Necessities of Daily Life
The class began with the traditional proverb "开门七件事" (Open the door, seven things) , which lists the essentials of daily life: firewood, rice, oil, salt, soy sauce, vinegar, and tea. This immediately established tea's central role in Chinese culture. The teacher then traced tea's origins back 5,000 years to the legendary Shennong (Divine Farmer), who, according to legend, discovered tea when leaves accidentally fell into boiling water.
The Vocabulary of Tea
The teacher introduced the character "茗" (míng) as a more poetic and literary term for tea, contrasting with everyday spoken Chinese "茶" (chá) . Students learned phrases such as:
•	品茗 (pǐn míng) – to savor tea;
•	香茗 (xiāng míng) – fragrant tea;
•	茗菜 (míng cài) – tea-infused dishes;
•	茗粥 (míng zhōu) – tea porridge.
The Seven Main Types of Chinese Tea
The teacher introduced the seven major categories of Chinese tea, each with distinct processing methods and flavor profiles:
•	Green tea (绿茶) – unfermented, the most popular category;
•	Black tea (红茶) – fully fermented (known as "red tea" in China);
•	Dark tea (黑茶) – post-fermented, including the famous Pu'er (普洱茶) ;
•	Oolong tea (乌龙茶) – semi-fermented;
•	White tea (白茶) – minimally processed;
•	Yellow tea (黄茶) – lightly fermented;
•	Flower tea (花茶) – scented with flowers (e.g., jasmine).
Tea Preparation Across Dynasties
The teacher provided a fascinating historical overview of how tea preparation evolved:
Han Dynasty (206 BC – 220 AD): Tea was prepared in large pots with additions of vegetables, meat, and other ingredients – more like a soup than the beverage we know today.
Tang Dynasty (618–907 AD): This was the golden age of tea, when the practice was elevated to an art form and spiritual ritual. The teacher introduced the legendary figure Lu Yu, who wrote the world's first tea encyclopedia, The Classic of Tea (茶经) , between 760–762 AD . Lu Yu, an orphan adopted by a Buddhist monk, became revered as the "God of Tea" (茶圣). His work covered everything from mountain cultivation to water temperature, transforming tea drinking into a sophisticated ritual .
During the Tang, tea was consumed as:
•	Pressed green tea bricks or cakes;
•	Roasted over fire, ground to powder in stone mortars, and sifted through silk;
•	Boiled with a pinch of salt, producing a frothy, jade-green brew resembling a soup .
The Tang also saw tea tastings at the imperial court, where aristocrats competed to describe the flavors most eloquently. Tea became a strategic commodity on the Silk Road and the Tea-Horse Road, traded for horses with Tibet and Central Asia. Poets like Li Bai and Du Fu drank tea to clear their minds, and tea became intertwined with Chan (Zen) Buddhism, as monks used it to stay awake during meditation .
Song Dynasty (960–1279 AD): Tea preparation became even more refined and elegant. The emperor himself wrote a book on tea, and the practice of drawing patterns on tea foam (a precursor to modern latte art) emerged.
Ming Dynasty (1368–1644 AD): An emperor declared that the tea ceremony had become too complex and expensive. He simplified the process to what we recognize today: just steeping loose tea leaves in boiled water.
Tea Utensils and Their Stories
The teacher introduced several culturally significant tea utensils:
•	六君子 (The Six Gentlemen) – six essential tools for tea preparation;
•	三才杯 (San Cai Cup) – a lidded cup representing the unity of heaven, earth, and humanity (the lid is heaven, the cup is earth, and the tea leaves represent humanity);
•	公道杯 (Gong Dao Cup) – the "fairness cup" used to distribute tea evenly among guests, ensuring everyone receives the same quality brew.
Practical Experience and Gifts
As a gesture of generosity, NEEPU presented each student with a complete tea cup set to take back home, allowing them to continue their journey into Chinese tea culture. The teacher then guided the students through a hands-on tea-making session, where they could practice the steps of:
•	Warming the cups;
•	Rinsing the tea leaves;
•	Steeping and serving.
Students had the opportunity to taste different types of tea that the teacher had brought, applying their newly acquired knowledge to distinguish flavors and aromas.
3. Summary and Reflections
July 3 was a day of remarkable contrasts and complementarity:
•	The morning nuclear power lecture provided deep engineering insights into one of the most advanced and safety-critical energy technologies. Students gained a thorough understanding of PWR systems, the multiple barriers protecting public safety, and China's nuclear development achievements.
•	The afternoon tea culture class offered a peaceful counterbalance, immersing students in the poetry, history, and ritual of tea. From Lu Yu's Classic of Tea to the Zen-inspired simplicity of the Ming Dynasty, students connected with the humanistic dimension of Chinese civilization.
The combination of technical rigor and cultural richness continues to define the summer camp experience, equipping the students with both professional knowledge and cross-cultural understanding.
